# AUTHOR LINE GROUPING — a COMMENT AT A CHILD'S CLOSE. Two costumes, and the
# second is this feature's own idiom.
#
# MUST EXCLUDE from any bulk `gg fmt` sweep: this fixture is a deliberately
# UNFORMATTED INPUT — the assertion is on what `gg fmt` turns it INTO
# (`fmt_author_rows_nested_comment_at_child_close`, exact text). A sweep would
# rewrite it into its own expected output and the cell would go green-vacuous.
#
# AXES CROSSED: the NESTING axis (a call's argument list; a nested container)
# × the COMMENT axis, at the position that discriminates them — the CHILD'S
# CLOSE. Its sibling `author_paren_holds_a_comment.gg` puts the comment inside an
# author GROUPING paren; here it sits inside a region whose delimiters belong to
# the element's own AST span. The two look almost identical in source and mean
# opposite things.
#
# ⚠ WHAT IT PINS. `author_line_break_between` reads the region between two
# elements' SPANS. A grouping paren's delimiters lie OUTSIDE the element's span
# (`parse_paren_expr` returns the wrapped node), so a comment between them breaks
# the outer row. A call, a tuple, a nested literal and a closure parameter list
# are all spanned INCLUDING their delimiters, so a comment inside them is inside
# the ELEMENT and the outer row must NOT break. Both outer rows below are
# therefore preserved exactly as written, comment and all.
#
# ⚠ AND IT PINS THE RATCHET, which is where this shape actually bit. The corpus
# row-fidelity guard has no AST, so it cannot tell these two apart; two
# successive syntactic proxies for the distinction were built and both fired on
# legal code — the second one on `xs` below, the very idiom this feature exists
# to serve. The guard now ABSTAINS on any site with a comment beneath it, and
# this fixture is what adjudicates the cell instead.
#
# RED-verified (Core #12): against the proxy build, the ratchet reported
# `1 author row signature(s) lost: [[1, 1, 2]]` for BOTH containers while
# `gg fmt` destroyed nothing — a fatal guard failing on correct code, whose only
# exits were reshaping the code or allowlisting a defect that did not exist.
from std.collections import Vector

int f(int a, int b):
    return a + b

void main():
    Vector[int] calls = [f(1, 2 # at the child close
), 20,
        30, 40,]
    Vector[Vector[int]] xs = [[1, 2, # top row
], [3, 4,],
        [5, 6,], [7, 8,],]
    print(calls.len() + xs.len())
